SELECT c.slug,'Education',
 'Lone Star Online Academy Closes, Leaving 12,000 Texas Students Seeking Schools',
 'The public virtual-school program connected to Roscoe Collegiate ISD confirmed it would not reopen for 2026–27 after a district contract decision. This historical page transparently reconstructs lost June 2026 coverage.',
 NULL::text,'Keep TX Red Newsroom','Lone Star Online Academy and public reporting','https://lsoa.k12.com/',
 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/file/Child_remote_schooling_on_a_laptop_in_the_countryside.jpg','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/file/Child_remote_schooling_on_a_laptop_in_the_countryside.jpg',
 'Illustrative 2023 image of a child using a laptop during remote study in the countryside; not a Lone Star Online Academy student or photograph of this closure.',
 'CruiseAmerica, Child remote schooling on a laptop in the countryside.jpg, photographed August 15, 2023, CC BY-SA 4.0 https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/. Source: https://www.cruiseamerica.com and Wikimedia Commons. Illustrative only, not the subject school.',
 '2026-06-30T16:00:00Z'::timestamptz,'news','{"updated":"2026-10-14T18:00:00Z","intro":["Lone Star Online Academy, a tuition-free public virtual school program connected to Roscoe Collegiate Independent School District, said it would not reopen for the 2026–27 academic year. Reporting published in late June 2026 described an abrupt closure affecting roughly 12,000 students who had expected to continue online classes. The school’s own enrollment website now confirms the closure and directs families toward other options.","The decision raised questions about academic oversight, the responsibilities of a public district that contracts with a private education company, and how families can receive timely help when a large remote-school program closes. The original June 30 Keep TX Red article body was lost; this is a sourced October 14 reconstruction preserving its historical URL, not a reproduction of the missing reporting."],"sections":[{"heading":"What happened and who ran the program","paragraphs":["Lone Star Online Academy at Roscoe was a public-school program of the Roscoe Collegiate Independent School District in West Texas, according to the academy’s official website. The district partnered with Stride K12, a private company that provides an online learning platform and related services. The program served students throughout Texas rather than only those living near Roscoe. That arrangement helps explain how a relatively small district came to oversee a virtual program serving thousands of children.","The Houston Chronicle reported on June 25 that the district decided not to renew its contract with Stride K12. Texas Standard examined the closure on June 30 and described how families and employees had received little warning after an earlier expectation that the school would continue. The closure is a confirmed change in the school''s operational status; it should not be confused with a statewide shutdown of virtual public education."]},{"heading":"Why the enrollment figure mattered","paragraphs":["Contemporary news accounts described approximately 12,000 affected students. That rounded figure represented an unusually large transition because families across Texas had to consider alternatives on a relatively short schedule. A public program’s enrollment can fluctuate between reporting periods; historical state-data profiles may show different totals. Those different snapshots should not be treated as contradictory without checking the date and definition used.","Online schooling is a distinct arrangement for families who need a remote option because of geography, health, scheduling, accessibility, or other individual circumstances. Enrollment in a virtual school is not simply interchangeable with signing up for an internet service; students need recognized course credit, appropriate instruction, student records and continuity of legally required support. Finding another school can be particularly complicated when the next semester is approaching."]},{"heading":"The role of performance and accountability","paragraphs":["The Chronicle linked the closure to the program’s history of low state accountability ratings and the district’s concerns about potential consequences. Its reporting described leaders discussing possible improvement from an F to a C before the closure announcement. These are attributed accounts, not a new independent calculation of student performance by Keep TX Red. The school’s official closure notice itself does not provide a detailed academic explanation of the district’s decision.","Public accountability ratings are one part of the picture, but the governance question is broader. A school district remains publicly accountable for the education provided under its name even when operational services are purchased from a private provider. Understanding which party made the contract decision, how families were notified and who holds student records is essential to assessing what went wrong and to improving closure procedures."]},{"heading":"What the district publicly considered in July","paragraphs":["A July 20, 2026 public notice for a Roscoe Collegiate ISD board meeting included an action item about formally closing the Lone Star Online Academy campus for the 2026–27 year and severing ties with K12/Stride. The public agenda establishes that the matter was scheduled for consideration. It does not by itself establish the precise vote, final adopted language or every contractual provision; those details require the approved minutes or other records.","The distinction between a decision already communicated to families and a later item on a board agenda matters. News coverage should not convert a public meeting notice into a claimed official vote without minutes. Families seeking official status should begin with the district and school’s published notices rather than assume that the presence of an agenda item resolves every administrative question."]},{"heading":"What parents and guardians can do","paragraphs":["The academy’s enrollment page says it will not open for the 2026–27 school year and provides a path for existing families to seek next steps and placement information. Parents should confirm that records, transcripts and any necessary course-completion information transfer to the receiving school. They should contact their intended district or program directly to confirm grade availability, admission timelines, instruction format and transfer procedures.","Families whose students receive special education services or accommodations should ask the receiving public-school system how evaluations, existing plans and services will be handled. A private-school offer is not necessarily equivalent to enrollment in a public-school program with all of the same legal protections. Because options and eligibility vary, an official district or Texas Education Agency contact is a better source for individualized decisions than a generalized news report."]},{"heading":"Why the case matters for Texas education policy","paragraphs":["The closure illustrates the difficulty of governing large statewide virtual programs through contracts anchored to a single public district. Students, parents, teachers, district boards, regulators and private vendors may each have different roles and obligations. Rapid enrollment growth can outpace the ability of families to understand which institution is responsible when service is interrupted.","Policy questions include how performance is measured for remote campuses, how quickly a district must tell families about a contract ending, how student records follow children, and whether the state has effective continuity plans. Those questions do not require assuming that online education is inherently unsuccessful; they require examining results, disclosure and contingency planning for the actual program."]},{"heading":"Reconstruction and photo attribution","paragraphs":["This article was reconstructed October 14, 2026 from the school’s own closure notice, Roscoe Collegiate ISD’s July board agenda and June reporting by the Houston Chronicle and Texas Standard. The original Keep TX Red June 30 news text is unavailable. The restoration keeps the same URL but does not invent quotations or imply that the original article was recovered word for word.","The accompanying licensed photograph depicts a child using a laptop while traveling in the countryside. It illustrates remote learning, not a student enrolled at Lone Star Online Academy, a Roscoe campus or an event connected to the closure. The photograph was published by CruiseAmerica under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0, with the source and license linked below."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Will Lone Star Online Academy open for the 2026–27 school year?","a":"No. The academy’s own website says it will not reopen for the 2026–27 school year."},{"q":"Was Lone Star Online Academy a public school?","a":"Yes. The academy was a public-school program of Roscoe Collegiate Independent School District that used services from Stride K12."},{"q":"How many students were affected by the closure?","a":"Contemporary June 2026 reporting described roughly 12,000 students; exact enrollment figures depend on the reporting period."},{"q":"Where should families ask about enrollment or student records?","a":"The academy’s official enrollment and closure page provides next steps, and families should contact the receiving school or public district for records and service continuity."}],"sources":[{"label":"Lone Star Online Academy official closure notice","url":"https://lsoa.k12.com/"},{"label":"Lone Star Online Academy official enrollment and transition page","url":"https://lsoa.k12.com/enrollment/"},{"label":"Roscoe Collegiate ISD board public notice, July 20","url":"https://meetings.boardbook.org/Public/PublicNotice/2397?meeting=756129"},{"label":"Houston Chronicle — Elizabeth Sander, June 25 reporting","url":"https://www.houstonchronicle.com/news/houston-texas/education/article/virtual-school-closes-abruptly-22319484.php"},{"label":"Texas Standard — Sarah Asch, June 30","url":"https://texasstandard.org/stories/lone-star-online-academy-closure-texas-school/"},{"label":"Wikimedia Commons — CruiseAmerica illustrative remote-learning photo CC BY-SA 4.0","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Child_remote_schooling_on_a_laptop_in_the_countryside.jpg"}],"keyTakeaways":["Lone Star Online Academy publicly confirmed it would not reopen for the 2026–27 school year.","June reporting described about 12,000 students affected and linked the closure to a district contract decision.","The original June 30 article wording was lost, so this page is a transparent historical reconstruction based on identified sources."]}'::jsonb,
